    <chapter title="Arrays of custom element types" id="array-literal">
        <tldr>
            <include from="lib.topic" element-id="jdbc-supported"/>
            <include from="lib.topic" element-id="r2dbc-supported"/>
        </tldr>
        <p>
            The <code>.array()</code> function relies on the driver's native array support, which only works for
            element types that the driver can encode and decode. For PostgreSQL arrays of other element types, like
            user-defined enum or composite types, use the
            <a href="https://jetbrains.github.io/Exposed/api/exposed-core/org.jetbrains.exposed.v1.core/-table/array-literal.html">
                <code>.arrayLiteral()</code>
            </a>
            function instead. It reads and writes the array in its text form (<code>{elem,elem,...}</code>), so it
            works the same way with both JDBC and R2DBC drivers.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each element is converted using the column type passed to <code>.arrayLiteral()</code>:
        </p>
        <list>
            <li>
                When writing, the element's database value (from <code>notNullValueToDB()</code>) is converted to a
                string, which must be a valid PostgreSQL input literal for the element type.
            </li>
            <li>
                When reading, each element is passed to <code>valueFromDB()</code> as a <code>String</code>.
            </li>
        </list>
        <p>
            For example, the following table stores an array of a PostgreSQL enum type, using the same column type
            that <a href="Enumeration-types.topic"><code>.customEnumeration()</code></a> uses:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="kotlin">
        // CREATE TYPE mood AS ENUM ('happy', 'sad');
        enum class Mood { HAPPY, SAD }

        object Diary : Table("diary") {
            val moods = arrayLiteral(
                "moods",
                CustomEnumerationColumnType(
                    name = "moods",
                    sql = "mood",
                    fromDb = { value -&gt; Mood.valueOf(value.toString().uppercase()) },
                    toDb = { it.name.lowercase() }
                )
            )
        }
        </code-block>
        <p>
            For a composite type, define a column type that renders and parses a single composite literal:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="kotlin">
        // CREATE TYPE member AS (id BIGINT, role TEXT);
        data class Member(val id: Long, val role: String)

        class MemberColumnType : ColumnType&lt;Member&gt;() {
            override fun sqlType(): String = "member"

            // For example: (1,"admin")
            override fun notNullValueToDB(value: Member): Any =
                "(${value.id},\"${value.role.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\"", "\\\"")}\")"

            override fun valueFromDB(value: Any): Member =
                parseMemberLiteral(value.toString()) // parse the composite literal, e.g. (1,admin)
        }

        object Teams : Table("teams") {
            val members = arrayLiteral("members", MemberColumnType())
        }
        </code-block>
        <p>
            Elements can be <code>null</code> if the element type of the list is nullable, for example
            <code>arrayLiteral&lt;Mood?&gt;(...)</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            PostgreSQL defines the character that separates array elements per element type. This is a comma for
            almost all types, but the built-in <code>box</code> type uses a semicolon, and user-defined base types can
            set their own. Use the <code>delimiter</code> parameter for such types:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="kotlin">
        val areas = arrayLiteral("areas", BoxColumnType(), delimiter = ';')
        </code-block>
        <note>
            <p>
                <code>.arrayLiteral()</code> is only supported by PostgreSQL, and only for one-dimensional arrays.
                JDBC drivers decode the array themselves when reading, so the element column type must also handle
                the element values they produce. For example, the pgjdbc-ng driver decodes composite elements into
                <code>java.sql.Struct</code> objects and <code>box</code> elements into <code>DoubleArray</code> objects.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Array indexing" id="array-indexing">
